Z||000018||Oreif||06-06-2001||02:23 AM||||You know that since you have an 84, I'm assuming it's the 4-cyl engine, The Indy scoop would put the hole right above the throttle body. With a K&N cone filter mounted straight up, the air would catch the scoop, curve down and go straight into the Throttle body. I would assume the flow would be better than directing it from the driver side and curving it to the TB using the stock air cleaner housing. Also it would probably look better than just one of the other scoops. <BR>Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000019||TONY_C||06-06-2001||09:21 AM||||Ram Air is a myth. At the speeds an auto is capable of going there is no "Ram" effect.  better off leaving the engine grates alone and let them do what they were designed for, letting warm air out. The side scoops are the way to get a cold, dense air charge to the engine. <A HREF="http://www.gemair.com/~phorine/Articles-Ram%20Air%20Myth.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gemair.com/~phorine/Articles-Ram%20Air%20Myth.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Formula88||06-06-2001||09:37 AM||||Well, I read the Ram Air myth (good article) and I've studied Mechanical Engineering in college (switched majors before graduation, though).  In theory, this all sounds correct.  But I have one example from real world testing to throw out.  The 70-72 Trans Am had a rear facing shaker hood scoop.  Why was it rear facing?  Because they found at racing speed, the forward facing scoop took in enough air to lean out the mixture too much.  A rear facing scoop still gets air forced in due to the high pressure area at the base of the windshield, but the pressure isn't as high as the front facing scoop was subjected to.<P>Now, this may not be "Ram Air" according to the physics, but the scoop increased the volume of air injested by the engine (hence the leaning out condition).  So at that point, Ram Air may be an advertising gimmick and misnomer, but the scoops do have an effect on performance.  This has been proven.  <P>I guess you can chalk this up to a case of the automotive industry taking an aerospace idea and using it for selling cars, whether or not it actually does anything.  Anyone remember the fins on a '59 Caddy?  At least automotive "Ram Air" does something for the vehicle's performance.||||reg||6
Z||000021||Fiero_24||06-06-2001||09:51 AM||||sjp777<BR>I was explaining that for cooling they would not work.  I know a few people that have them thinking that they will cool the car.  I changed the post just to please you.   <p>[This message has been edited by Fiero_24 (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||Ken Wittlief||06-06-2001||10:02 AM||||This would be a simple argument to prove. Put a vacuum/pressure gauge on the throttle body , right infront of the butterfly.<P>  drive the car with the scoop / ram air / duct work off, and with it inplace, at 60, 80, 100 mph.<P>  If you have higher airpressure at the throttle body inlet (or less vacuum), then you are ramming air into it, instead of forcing the engine to suck it all in.<P>   its the same idea as a turbo or a blower.<P>  the trans am may have leaned out, will the ECM on the fiero recognize the lean O2 readings and increase fuel commands to the injectors? maybe? maybe it will work on the fiero.<P>  If you are gonna mess with scoops and hoods, put the vacuum/pressure gauage in there and get some real numbers so you can 'see' what you are doing.<P>   BTW thanks for the historical info on the T/A. I always wondered who the bone head was that put the hood scoop on backwards. Now I know its the bone head who couldnt be bothered to get the mixture right to match all the added airflow.<P>BTW if you do mess around with ram air, remeber it will take your ECM 30 miles or more at that speed to fully adjust and store the data for the new conditions. In might even be better to reset the ECM to make it adapt to the new intake air flow.||||reg||1
Z||000023||fyerstarter||06-06-2001||12:48 PM||||The problem with that and problem the same problem with the TA (which I dont think had a computer back then) is that while your driving slow or stopped then there is not ram air, and it is exponentially greater as speed increases. I think for it to work properly there would have to be some sort or chip that could adjust air/fuel on the fly such as you use with some nitrous setups, and with turbos.<P>Also what about the new TA WS6? I'm not sure but I think the only difference in this and the next TA down is the ram air option, and it boast 10-20 more horsepower. ||||reg||1
Z||000024||fyerstarter||06-06-2001||12:49 PM||||Hehe oh, I just read the ram air myth article. I guess there goes the theory about the TA Ws6 :P<P>Wow, what an informative article that is. My only argument would be that colder air is denser so even if the ram air effect does nothing to compress the air itself it still provides a colder and denser source of air from outside the car for the intake to take  in. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by fyerstarter (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Ken Wittlief||06-06-2001||02:56 PM||||I hope whoever wrote that article did it as a term paper, and got the appropriate F for it.<P>  air is not compressable below mach 0.5?! so I guess planes cannot fly slower than 350mph cause the airpressure on the tops and bottoms and the side of the wings and tail surfaces would all be equal.<P>   somebody better call the airport quick<P>   the air pressure on the front of your car is higher than the air pressure on the back of it, thats why you have to expend about 8HP to keep your car moving at a steady speed at 60mph.<P>  I guess the author has never pulled a wing vent all the way backwards on an older car and felt the blast of air that hits your face at 70mph - and has never tried to breath on a motorcycle with an open faced helmet at 80 with your mouth open. <P>   If air is moving there is a pressure differential. I think its time for this guy to put his math book down and get his hands greasy. The fact that turning the scoop backwards on the older T/As fixed the lean-out problem is another proof that he is full of beans.<P>   BTW - someone correct me if I am wrong, but I think the ECM on the fiero maps fuel ratios for different rpms, temps, speeds... so I think it would correct the ratio with air RAMMED into the intake manifold - within the range of adjustment that it is able to make.<P>  again, this whole thing can be settled with a Kmart pressure / vacuum gauge and a hood scoop.||||reg||1
Z||000026||cadaver||06-06-2001||06:15 PM||||Ok now we get into boundry level airflow and low pressure areas. Like the T/A scoops. They were not made to ram the air. Ever look at a scoop on a plane where air flow is a real important thing. I will snap some and post them if interested. <BR>Hey grinth READ THE POSTS FIRST||||reg||1
Z||000027||Oreif||06-06-2001||07:19 PM||||I just read the "Ram Air" article. First off why is it that a turbo and supercharger provide the ram air affect  but a scoop on a car can't? Turbo's and SC's provide boost. From 0 to 15 pounds generated by either exhaust or belt-driven impeller. If you take a boost gauge and hook it up to the air intake tube of a Fiero and drive the car at 70mph the gauge shows negative boost. With a holley scoop it stays close to 0 boost. So that tells me the holley isn't a "ram air" scoop but it does change the intake pressure to a positive pressure at highway speeds. Now take the same tube and attach it to an over-the-roof style scoop and read the boost gauge. HMM it shows about 1.5 - 2 pounds of boost. So if there is a measureable amount of boost going into the engine then you have "Ram Air" (The above was done by Huffaker when they were building and testing the original Fiero race cars) Second, He states if you increase either air <B> OR </B> fuel that there is no gain. This is true. You can actually lose performance. (too lean or too rich mixture) But on Fiero's there is a Manifold Air Pressure sensor. This tell the ECM what the pressure is in the manifold and adjusts fuel accordingly. On the older cars with carbs. The more air that passes thru the venturi's also draws out more fuel. So in both cases if air flow/pressure is increased so is fuel. I don't think that the gain of 1 - 2 pounds of boost at 70mph is going to make the car any "faster" but the engine would not have to work as hard to maintain the speed. Also the cooler air will aid the engine by keeping the intake manifold temps cooler. <P>I know for a fact the Holley scoop will increase gas mileage on long highway speed trips. I did 3 trips (300 miles each way) with my first Fiero and the second trip had the holley scoop on it. The first and third did not. I gained almost 4 miles per gallon with the scoop. This is only on the highway. City driving had no change and there was no perfomance difference. It isn't "Ram Air" but it helps slightly.||||reg||1
Z||000028||sjp777||06-06-2001||08:22 PM||||So There!<P>Thanks Oreif<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg"> <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000029||theogre||06-06-2001||08:45 PM||||If you are looking for engine bay cooling. I think side scoopes are better. Use the left for engine air and the right for cooling air. This should help cool the belts and things more. ((Keep in mind, Fiero engine bay can easily reach 200F. That kind of heat doesn't play well with plastic and rubber products.))<P>The problem I have with overhead intakes, especially for cooling, is that under certain conditions the air movement thru them will be zero because you have hot air trying to rise and low vehicle speed blocking it in. At highway speed I'm not sure you ram enough air down to adequately cool the bay. That depends on the specific overhead scoop used. I wish I could find a set nearby so I could put a pyrometer in the bay and test them with the over heads on and off.<P>One good thing about overheads is that they should act like chimneys when you park. That would help draft the heat out of the bay faster and prevent cooking of the wiring and belts.<P>You can't just slap on side vents and expect the to do a whole lot. You need to do some work behind them to make sure the air goes where you need it. Otherwise a large portion is simply blowing around the wheel wells and fenders not into the engine intake or the bay.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Ken Wittlief||06-07-2001||09:39 AM||||To give the author of the article a little credit, its possible the phrase 'ram air' means something very specific to aerospace or jet engine designers, so maybe - technically speaking - you cant get 'ram air' on a car.<P>   but that doesnt mean putting a scoop into the windstream does not improve airflow into the intake manifold or into the cooling space.<P>   common sense does apply here. Open the windows on your car and drive around, then put a scoop out into the airflow and direct its output at your face.<P>  if you are the engine, on a hot day you will be cooler with that air blast hitting you.<P>   One thing you do have to be aware of with scoops and ducts is laminar flow. Thats then you turn on the sink and the water from the faucet hits a spoon and fans off in a sheet all over your pants. If you can maintain a laminar air flow like that through the scoop and direct it towards the part of the engine you most want to cool, it has a tremenous effect on temp.<P>   If the laminar flow breaks down into turbulance, then you are only 'pumping' air through the engine compartment - not nearly as effective - but still better than nothing.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Oreif||06-07-2001||11:27 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B><BR>The problem I have with overhead intakes, especially for cooling, is that under certain conditions the air movement thru them will be zero because you have hot air trying to rise and low vehicle speed blocking it in. At highway speed I'm not sure you ram enough air down to adequately cool the bay. That depends on the specific overhead scoop used. I wish I could find a set nearby so I could put a pyrometer in the bay and test them with the over heads on and off.<P> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I took a temperature gauge and set the sensor just above the intake plenum on my car. Without the Indy scoop at idle after driving, the temp was around 170F while city driving it was about 160F. Highway was 155F With the Indy scoop on at idle was about 165F but city driving was 130F at 60mph on a long highway drive (35 minutes of driving at 65mph) it stabilizes at 115F. The cooling is better over 30mph. At idle you can feel the heat coming out of the face of the scoop. Even when you park you can feel the heat coming out as the engine cools down. Mine blows down the rear side of the engine. The day I did this it was about 65F outside temp and partly sunny. On a hot summer day sitting in traffic I can see the temp gauge creep up to 200F but once I get moving over 30mph the temp drops quickly back to normal. The face of the Indy scoop is 14" X 2.5" and 2" into the wind stream. It is pointed to the backside of the engine so I am assuming with the air moving up the front side and the scoop pushing down the back side I have constantly moving air through out the entire engine bay.<BR>So at highway speeds it cools very well. But speeds below 30mph it is marginal. I think the "chiminey" effect is what hinders the cooling at low speeds.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||Ken Wittlief||06-07-2001||02:04 PM||||All this air blowing around the engine compartment is good for the accesories, wiring, bekts...<P>  but I would expect it to have almost zero effect on internal engine temp. your engine is liquid cooled, by the radiator up front.<P>  Dont expect side scoops to have any effect on engine coolant temp.<P>   and when you park, the 170 or 180° core temp of the motor heat-soaks the engine compartment - thats when you need those top vents opened there to let the heat out.||||reg||1
Z||000033||Oreif||06-07-2001||02:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B><BR>  but I would expect it to have almost zero effect on internal engine temp. your engine is liquid cooled, by the radiator up front.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The internal engine temp does change slightly by air. If an engine is sitting in 170F room and another is in a 120F room with a fan blowing, the engine in the 120 room will run cooler. Not because the air cools it but because the radiant heat around the outside of the engine is being removed quicker. ||||reg||1
Z||000034||Ken Wittlief||06-07-2001||02:50 PM||||I agree it has some effect - but I dont think its significant<P>  certainly not if you drained your coolant and tried to drive.<BR>  <BR>  picture two gallons of gasoline burning in a coleman stove in one hour - thats how much heat your engine produces - it doesnt have enought surface area to cool much by radiation.<P>   some engineer fanatic could do the thermodynamic equations here, but you get the idea. 2 gallons of gas in an hour makes for one hellatious blowtorch of a heat source.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Jncomutt||06-07-2001||03:59 PM||||So, back to the question, after all is said and done, No one knows where ta find em huh?  Oh well... Guess this means moving on to plan B, contacting IRM.<P>Oh yea, Anyone know their website??<P>------------------<BR>17 year old Fierophile<BR>'86 Coupe 160k miles<BR>'84 Mint Beauty 51k and original tires!||||reg||1
Z||000036||Oreif||06-07-2001||06:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jncomutt:<BR><B>So, back to the question, after all is said and done, No one knows where ta find em huh?  Oh well... Guess this means moving on to plan B, contacting IRM.<P>Oh yea, Anyone know their website??<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They don't have a website.<BR>Their Email is: IRMFIERO@aol.com<BR>I don't have the phone # right now.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000037||ThatRickGuy||06-08-2001||10:16 AM||||Just to toss in my $.02, the reversed scoops and cowls work off Static preasure. I can understand w/ the writer was trying to say, but I don't think he did a good job of saying it. It's dynamic preasure that holds planes up. Think of it this way, Dynamic presure pushes in 1 direction, Static pushes in all direction. Anyways, Ram air isn't totally defunked like the guy said, at the least, it's an improved air flow and cool looking.||||reg||1
Z||000038||Ken Wittlief||06-08-2001||11:05 AM||||it is cool looking, and thats important too.<P>we all know the wings on our engine lids have no effect below 90mph, except to increase drag and lower our mpg<P>  but we like em anyway.<P>  ill bet the guy that wrote that article has some save the globe - anti HP muscle car thing going on.<P>  probabally drives a golf cart to school at UCLA.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000039||Slammed Fiero||06-08-2001||11:26 AM||||hahah Ram Air  is a joke.<P>Gm Brought it back to "Excite" the market place. Target market is roughly 25 to 35 years old. All these people remember the ram air systems of the 60's. It's a huge marketing ploy. The extra HP created by the newer version of ram air is by the exhaust.<P>I don't see how side scoops make a difference on a dyno , there is no air flowing past them to be forced in.. IT's all BS to sell parts. I wanna see dyno slips. Anyone who has built any sort of cold air induction has lost Torque and gained a weeee bit of Hp , perhaps 2. (Shaun41178 where are you)<P>When I put my mustang scoop on my car I was running a 2 day event at shannonville (2.8km roadcourse) I have a pyrometer at my disposal. Putting the scoop on to vent the hot air out worked to lower engine temps after a few hard laps. The idea that these scoops work and create HP is a joke , espically on a dyno , there is no air pressure available , except for the fan they put infront of the car to cool the rad.<P>I will see if I can track down Fiero owner/ Autocrosser Steve huskins who is a Aerospace engineer. We had this discussion about 4 years ago on the .org peter zurich list. <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000040||Ken Wittlief||06-08-2001||01:20 PM||||well of course its not gonna show any difference on a dyno, unless maybe it drops HP some cause it has to suck air in trough the scoop.<P>   Heres another simple test. Run with and without scoops on a straight and level test track, WFO, both directions, and average the top speeds.<P>  you cant argue with HP =&gt; top speed as long as you dont change anything else on the car.<P>  we can debate the physics of it all day long. Are we a bunch of moldy old guys who sit around talking fieros all day long<P>  or do we actually drive the things once in a while too?||||reg||1
Z||000041||Tigger||06-09-2001||11:52 PM||||Ok, according to that article, these guys have it all wrong?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/john_force.jpg"> <BR>John Force/Austin Coil/Bernie Fedderly<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/monaco18.jpg"> <BR>Michael Schumacher/Every F1-IRL-Cart teams<P>Something, for some strange reason, is telling me they don't run "Ram Air" scoops just because it looks cool.||||reg||10
Z||000042||Pontiaddict||06-10-2001||01:07 AM||||The air venting around the engine compartment isn't about engine cooling, it's about keeping all of that electronic stuff cool. Heat ruins coils, alternators, fuel injectiors, ignition modules, batteries, and lots of other nifty electrical gizmos found on Fieros.<BR>||||reg||1
